#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f481bacc-75b7-525d-8784-9e5138117597" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as an Improper Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "68ab3d23-d9c7-5037-9ff5-1803bee39a02" :question/math-academy-id "e-13826" :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{3} &= \\frac{3}{3} \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{7}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142621 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8e4d9a7-afea-562f-b013-9c02fbaf3455" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Result is a Whole Number"}] :question/id #uuid "1130bb19-8742-53c5-b07c-22c310f1d927" :question/math-academy-id "e-13829" :question/problem "What is the value of $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1{3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} &= \\frac{3}{3} \\cdot \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142623 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0261453-d071-5dbf-8d63-39c77ab39c72" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as a Mixed Number"}] :question/id #uuid "b93f9bde-7033-529a-a8fa-63066c95d48e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13834" :question/problem "What is $3 \\, \\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1 \\,\\dfrac 2 {5}$ expressed as a mixed number?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac 1{2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}= \\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 5)+2}{5}= \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 {2} \\times \\dfrac{7}{5} = \\dfrac {7\\times 7} {2\\times 5} = \\dfrac{49}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{49}{10} =4\\,\\textrm{R} 9 = 4\\,\\dfrac 9 {10}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142625 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6340c1e2-bf12-5932-9632-df7b72f499d9" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as a Mixed Number (Solution Uses a Strategy)"}] :question/id #uuid "1dcc8e5a-21ab-52af-b9ef-fccb97d74eb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13838" :question/problem "Express $2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} \\times 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}$ as a mixed number in its lowest terms." :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1{4}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}= \\dfrac {9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3} \\cdot \\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{3} \\cdot \\frac{9}{4} &= \\frac{7}{4} \\cdot \\frac{9}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{4} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{21}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{21}{4} = 5\\,\\textrm{R} 1= 5\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0261453-d071-5dbf-8d63-39c77ab39c72" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as a Mixed Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "f210ec43-c635-5084-a3ae-0f28eb902bdb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140333}] :question/id #uuid "f22f500c-8689-5ce5-a371-3cafba346f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-171727" :question/problem "Expressed as a mixed number in its lowest terms, $3\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1\\frac{2}{3} =$ {{field-1}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f481bacc-75b7-525d-8784-9e5138117597" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as an Improper Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d52826c-24eb-5ebf-975f-0963a3439ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142466} {:answer/id #uuid "e326b6dd-301e-56fc-a530-84d7034f780f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142467} {:answer/id #uuid "086ee004-fad2-573e-aadb-7f6004e5e2b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/iden